development.question_text:
This factor tree for $70$ is incorrect. Explain the mistake and give the correct prime factorisation.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\node (a) at (0,2) {$70$};
\node (b) at (-1.2,1) {$7$};
\node (c) at (1.2,1) {$10$};
\node (d) at (0.4,0) {$5$};
\node (e) at (2,0) {$5$};
\draw (a)--(b);
\draw (a)--(c);
\draw (c)--(d);
\draw (c)--(e);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

development.solution_text:
$5\times 5=25$, not $10$, so the split of the $10$ is wrong. The prime factors of $10$ are $2$ and $5$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\node (a) at (0,2) {$70$};
\node (b) at (-1.2,1) {$7$};
\node (c) at (1.2,1) {$10$};
\node (d) at (0.4,0) {$2$};
\node (e) at (2,0) {$5$};
\draw (a)--(b);
\draw (a)--(c);
\draw (c)--(d);
\draw (c)--(e);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$70$
$=2\times 5\times 7$

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\node (a) at (0,2) {$24$};
\node (b) at (-1.4,1) {$4$};
\node (c) at (1.4,1) {$6$};
\node (d) at (-2.2,0) {$2$};
\node (e) at (-0.6,0) {$2$};
\node (f) at (0.6,0) {$2$};
\node (g) at (2.2,0) {$3$};
\draw (a)--(b);
\draw (a)--(c);
\draw (b)--(d);
\draw (b)--(e);
\draw (c)--(f);
\draw (c)--(g);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}
\node (a) at (0,3) {$24$};
\node (b) at (-1.6,2) {$3$};
\node (c) at (1.6,2) {$8$};
\node (d) at (0.6,1) {$2$};
\node (e) at (2.6,1) {$4$};
\node (f) at (1.9,0) {$2$};
\node (g) at (3.3,0) {$2$};
\draw (a)--(b);
\draw (a)--(c);
\draw (c)--(d);
\draw (c)--(e);
\draw (e)--(f);
\draw (e)--(g);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The first tree gives $2\times 2\times 2\times 3$ and the second gives $3\times 2\times 2\times 2$.
$24=2^{3}\times 3$
The trees differ but the prime factorisation is the same, because it is unique.

